[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: DNS service (Bind9) on srv1. PC1’s resolver is pointed at srv1 (10.5.0.4), but that DNS server is refusing recursive queries from PC1’s subnet.

Mechanism: When PC1 tries to resolve names, srv1 responds “REFUSED” (recursion not available). Applications then stall while retrying or falling back (e.g., to DoH or cached IPs). That creates the “hang for a bit, then it comes back” behavior, even though basic IP connectivity is fine.

Localization and evidence:
- PC1 network is healthy: ping to default gateway 10.1.0.1 is clean (0% loss, ~0.6 ms), and internet reachability via the lab “internet” IP 216.239.35.4 is fine from both PC1 and r1 (low RTT).
- PC1 uses DNS 10.5.0.4 (srv1) per /etc/resolv.conf.
- From PC1: nslookup/host example.com against 10.5.0.4 returns “recursion not available … REFUSED.”
- On srv1, Bind’s options show no forwarders and default policy; with default allow-recursion limited to localhost/localnets (10.5.0.0/24), clients in 10.1.0.0/24 like PC1 are denied.

Root cause: Bind9 on srv1 is misconfigured to deny recursion for user VLANs, while clients are configured to use it for DNS.

Corrective fix: On srv1, enable recursion for campus subnets (e.g., in named.conf.options add:
allow-recursion { 10.0.0.0/8; }; allow-query-cache { 10.0.0.0/8; }; recursion yes;
and/or add forwarders to upstream resolvers), then restart bind9. Alternatively, update DHCP/client resolvers to a DNS that permits recursion. This will remove the intermittent delays immediately. 
=== END ===